  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nWeather conditions across the forecast area early this morning have \nfeatured isolated showers and a few thunderstorms ahead of a cold \nfront that has pushed into central SD. Gusty northwest winds have \ndeveloped behind this front across central and western SD where \nobserved gusts have reached 45-50 mph in some locations. Cooler and \ndrier air has already begun to filter into central sections of the \nstate where temperatures in the upper 40s to low 50s have taken hold \nwhile our eastern zones are still holding onto the 60s.\n\nThe cold front will continue to shift east across our eastern \nforecast area and is progged to clear our CWA by mid to late \nmorning. Guidance progs an increase in shower activity post fropa \nacross our eastern zones this morning into the first half of the \nafternoon before tapering off. The strongest winds gusts are still \nexpected to set up west of the James Valley where speeds will range \nbetween 40-50 mph through about late morning. Wind speeds are then \nexpected to gradually diminish this afternoon. A Wind Advisory will \nremain posted for most of central SD until 15Z. High pressure will \nslowly build into the area from the northwest this afternoon into \ntonight. Parts of central SD will have a better chance of seeing \nsome clearing as a result. A much cooler, more seasonal air mass \nwill take over today with daytime temperatures in the 50s to low \n60s. Tonight will feature some scattered clearing early, otherwise \nclouds will persist or thicken overnight and despite this overnight \nlows are expected to fall into the low to mid 30s.\n\nSouthwest flow is progged to remain the dominant upper flow pattern \nacross the Northern Plains this weekend through the middle of next \nweek. We will see a brief lull in rain chances tonight into Sunday \nmorning. An upper trough is progged to kick northeast into our \nregion late this weekend through Monday as an more broad upper low \ntracks into the Pacific Northwest. Lead shortwave energy shifting \ninto SD on Sunday will kick off rain chances across southern SD. \nWith the sfc high poised to our north and northeast, a drier fetch \nof air in the lower levels should effectively keep most of the rain \nout of our northern and eastern zones most of the daytime hours on \nSunday. Once the main shortwave trough axis lifts overhead Sunday \nnight through the first half of Tuesday, more widespread rain chances \nexpand north and east. Rainfall probabilities of at least a half \ninch or more are fairly high across our southern and eastern zones \nwhere they range between 60-90 percent. Farther to the north and \nwest(north central SD), probabilities of these amounts range between \n30-60 percent. Temperatures will actually be near to below normal \nthrough the early portion of next week with daytime highs struggling \nto reach 50 degrees on Monday into Tuesday. Overnight lows will be \nnear or just below the freezing mark. This could lead to the \npotential for some wet snowflakes to mix in with the rain across \nnorth central SD Monday into Monday night.",
